  the wives with shawls pulled tight,
  the children told to watch for a red hull,
  the dogs who knew before anyone.
  The fog held all of them, too,
  cool against their faces,
  patient as a held breath.

  By noon the sun will burn it off.
  The boats will remember their names.
  The far shore will come back,
  smug and solid, as if it never left.

  But for now the world is small enough
  to carry in two hands:
  this wet stone, this one gull,
  this lamp somebody left burning,
  and the long low voice of the bell
  saying [3mhere, here, here[0m
  to anyone still out there listening.

